[section:laguerre Laguerre (Associated and Generalized) Polynomials]

[h4 Synopsis]

``
#include <boost/math/special_functions/laguerre.hpp>
``

   namespace boost{ namespace math{
   
   template <class T>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, T x);
   
   template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, T x, const ``__Policy``&);
   
   template <class T>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, unsigned m, T x);
   
   template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, unsigned m, T x, const ``__Policy``&);

   template <class T1, class T2>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, T1 alpha, T2 x);

   template <class T1, class T2, class ``__Policy``>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, T1 alpha, T2 x, const ``__Policy``&);
   
   template <class T1, class T2, class T3>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre_next(unsigned n, T1 x, T2 Ln, T3 Lnm1);
   
   template <class T1, class T2, class T3>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre_next(unsigned n, unsigned m, T1 x, T2 Ln, T3 Lnm1);

   template <class T>
   std::vector<T> laguerre_zeros(unsigned n, T alpha = 0);

   template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
   std::vector<T> laguerre_zeros(unsigned n, T alpha, const ``__Policy``&);

   
   }} // namespaces
   
[h4 Description]

The return type of these functions is computed using the __arg_promotion_rules:
note than when there is a single template argument the result is the same type 
as that argument or `double` if the template argument is an integer type.

[optional_policy]

   template <class T>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, T x);
   
   template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, T x, const ``__Policy``&);
   
Returns the value of the Laguerre Polynomial of order /n/ at point /x/:

[equation laguerre_0]

The following graph illustrates the behaviour of the first few 
Laguerre Polynomials:

[graph laguerre]
   
   template <class T>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, unsigned m, T x);
   
   template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, unsigned m, T x, const ``__Policy``&);
   
Returns the Associated Laguerre polynomial of degree /n/ 
and order /m/ at point /x/:

[equation laguerre_1]

   template <class T1, class T2>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, T1 alpha, T2 x);

   template <class T1, class T2, class ``__Policy``>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre(unsigned n, T1 alpha, T2 x, const ``__Policy``&);

When /alpha/ has a floating-point type, returns the generalized Laguerre polynomial of degree /n/ and parameter [alpha] at /x/,

/L/[sub /n/][super ([alpha])](/x/) = [sum][sub /k/=0][super /n/] (-1)[super /k/] C(/n/ + [alpha], /n/ - /k/) /x/[super /k/] / /k/!,

which is orthogonal for the weight /x/[super [alpha]] e[super -/x/] on \[0, [infin]) when [alpha] > -1, but is a polynomial for any real [alpha].
For an integer [alpha] = /m/ it is the associated polynomial above, and for [alpha] = 0 the Laguerre polynomial.
The result type is computed from both arguments; an integer order /m/ leaves it to /x/ alone.
Its zeros are given by `laguerre_zeros`, below.
   
   template <class T1, class T2, class T3>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre_next(unsigned n, T1 x, T2 Ln, T3 Lnm1);
   
Implements the three term recurrence relation for the Laguerre
polynomials, this function can be used to create a sequence of
values evaluated at the same /x/, and for rising /n/.

[equation laguerre_2]

For example we could produce a vector of the first 10 polynomial
values using:

   double x = 0.5;  // Abscissa value
   vector<double> v;
   v.push_back(laguerre(0, x)).push_back(laguerre(1, x));
   for(unsigned l = 1; l < 10; ++l)
      v.push_back(laguerre_next(l, x, v[l], v[l-1]));
      
Formally the arguments are:

[variablelist
[[n][The degree /n/ of the last polynomial calculated.]]
[[x][The abscissa value]]
[[Ln][The value of the polynomial evaluated at degree /n/.]]
[[Lnm1][The value of the polynomial evaluated at degree /n-1/.]]
]
   
   template <class T1, class T2, class T3>
   ``__sf_result`` laguerre_next(unsigned n, unsigned m, T1 x, T2 Ln, T3 Lnm1);

Implements the three term recurrence relation for the Associated Laguerre
polynomials, this function can be used to create a sequence of
values evaluated at the same /x/, and for rising degree /n/.

[equation laguerre_3]

For example we could produce a vector of the first 10 polynomial
values using:

   double x = 0.5;  // Abscissa value
   int m = 10;      // order
   vector<double> v;
   v.push_back(laguerre(0, m, x)).push_back(laguerre(1, m, x));
   for(unsigned l = 1; l < 10; ++l)
      v.push_back(laguerre_next(l, m, x, v[l], v[l-1]));
      
Formally the arguments are:

[variablelist
[[n][The degree of the last polynomial calculated.]]
[[m][The order of the Associated Polynomial.]]
[[x][The abscissa value.]]
[[Ln][The value of the polynomial evaluated at degree /n/.]]
[[Lnm1][The value of the polynomial evaluated at degree /n-1/.]]
]

`laguerre_zeros` returns the /n/ zeros of the generalized Laguerre polynomial /L/[sub /n/][super ([alpha])], in increasing order;
they are all positive. For /n/ = 0 the result is empty.
/L/[sub /n/][super ([alpha])] is orthogonal for the weight /x/[super [alpha]] e[super -/x/] on \[0, [infin]),
and for integer [alpha] = /m/ it is the associated polynomial `laguerre(n, m, x)` above.
[alpha] must be finite and greater than -1, otherwise the result is a vector of /n/ values returned by __domain_error.
The type /T/ must be a floating-point type: write `laguerre_zeros<double>(n)` or `laguerre_zeros(n, 0.5)`.
These are the abscissas of the [link math_toolkit.gauss_laguerre Gauss-Laguerre quadrature] rule.

The zeros are found in O(/n/) operations by following the differential equation of the Laguerre functions
from zero to zero in twice the working precision (Glaser, Liu and Rokhlin).
Where that cannot vouch for a zero, the zeros come from the three-term recurrence instead, which overflows for large /n/ combined with
large [alpha] (in `double`, for example /n/ = 2000 and [alpha] = 50): the result is then a vector of values returned by __evaluation_error.
In our tests against high-precision values they are correctly rounded in `float`, `double` and 50-digit precision.
   
[h4 Accuracy]

The following table shows peak errors (in units of epsilon) 
for various domains of input arguments.  
Note that only results for the widest floating point type on the system are 
given as narrower types have __zero_error.

[table_laguerre_n_x_]

[table_laguerre_n_m_x_]

Near a root the relative error grows with the condition number |/x/ /L/'(/x/) / /L/(/x/)|, as for any
method that is given /x/ rounded to the working precision.

[h4 Testing]

A mixture of spot tests of values calculated using functions.wolfram.com, 
and randomly generated test data are
used: the test data was computed using
[@http://shoup.net/ntl/doc/RR.txt NTL::RR] at 1000-bit precision.

[h4 Implementation]

These functions are implemented using the three term
recurrence relations. On its own the recurrence loses up to about /n/[super 1.5] [epsilon] where the polynomial oscillates,
so the rounding error of each step, and of its coefficients 2/c/ + 1 + [alpha] - /x/ and /c/ + [alpha], is found
almost exactly and carried forward by the same recurrence. In tests against 600-bit sums the Laguerre, associated
and generalized polynomials are then correctly rounded in `double`, for degrees up to 200 and the arguments tested,
at about 10 ns per degree.

[endsect][/section:beta_function The Beta Function]
